# ---------------- Quoting ----------------
{
 "slug":"estimate-vs-firm-quote","tag":"Quoting",
 "c1":"#3730a3","c2":"#6366f1","oglabel":"Quoting",
 "ogline1":"Estimate vs","ogline2":"Firm Quote","ogtag":"free quotes, no sign-up",
 "subject":"two pricing documents and a pen on a desk",
 "title":"Estimate vs Firm Quote: What&rsquo;s the Difference?",
 "ogtitle":"Estimate vs Firm Quote",
 "cardh2":"Estimate vs Firm Quote",
 "carddesc":"An estimate is a best guess; a firm quote is a fixed commitment. Here is the difference and how to make clear which you&rsquo;re giving.",
 "desc":"Estimate vs firm quote: the difference between an approximate estimate and a binding fixed quote, when to use each, and how to make clear which one you're giving so a client doesn't hold a guess to the penny.",
 "lead":"An <b>estimate</b> and a <b>firm quote</b> sound similar but commit you to very different things: one is a best guess, the other a fixed price you&rsquo;re bound to. Confusing them causes disputes. Here is the difference.",
 "sections":[
  {"h2":"The key difference","html":"<p>An <b>estimate</b> is an <b>approximate</b> figure &mdash; your best assessment of likely cost, which may move as the job becomes clearer. A <b>firm quote</b> is a <b>fixed commitment</b>: accept it and that&rsquo;s the price, barring agreed changes. The difference is whether the number can move.</p>"},
  {"h2":"When to use each","html":"<ul><li>Use an <b>estimate</b> when the scope is unclear or the work is exploratory</li><li>Use a <b>firm quote</b> when you can scope the job confidently and the client wants certainty</li></ul><p>Giving a firm quote on genuinely unpredictable work is how you end up absorbing overruns.</p>"},
  {"h2":"Label it clearly","html":"<p>The danger is a client treating your <b>estimate as a quote</b> and holding you to the penny. So say which it is, in writing, on the document &mdash; &ldquo;<b>Estimate</b> (approximate, subject to change)&rdquo; vs &ldquo;<b>Quotation</b> (fixed for 30 days)&rdquo;. A word of clarity now prevents an argument later.</p>"},
  {"h2":"If an estimate changes","html":"<p>When an estimate needs to move, tell the client <b>as soon as you know</b>, not at invoicing. Explain what changed and give an updated figure. Clients accept a well-communicated change to an estimate; what they won&rsquo;t accept is a surprise on the final bill.</p>"},
 ],
 "faq":[
  {"q":"What's the difference between an estimate and a quote?","a":"An estimate is an approximate figure that may change as the job becomes clearer. A firm quote is a fixed price you're committed to once accepted, barring agreed changes. The difference is whether the number can move."},
  {"q":"When should I give an estimate instead of a quote?","a":"Give an estimate when the scope is unclear or the work is exploratory, and a firm quote when you can scope the job confidently and the client wants a fixed price."},
  {"q":"How do I stop a client treating my estimate as a fixed price?","a":"Label it clearly in writing &mdash; 'Estimate (approximate, subject to change)' versus 'Quotation (fixed)' &mdash; and if an estimate needs to move, tell the client as soon as you know, not at invoicing."},
 ],
 "related":[{"href":"/en/estimate","label":"Create a quote"},{"href":"/en/blog/estimate-vs-quote","label":"Estimate vs quote"},{"href":"/en/blog/how-to-write-a-quote","label":"How to write a quote"}],
 "cta_href":"/en/estimate","cta_label":"Create a quote free",
},
{
 "slug":"can-i-charge-more-than-i-quoted","tag":"Quoting",
 "c1":"#3730a3","c2":"#6366f1","oglabel":"Quoting",
 "ogline1":"Can I Charge More","ogline2":"Than I Quoted?","ogtag":"free quotes, no sign-up",
 "subject":"a quote document with a calculator and notes",
 "title":"Can I Charge More Than I Quoted?",
 "ogtitle":"Can I Charge More Than I Quoted?",
 "cardh2":"Charging Above the Quote",
 "carddesc":"The job grew beyond the quote. Can you bill more? Here is when you can, how to handle it, and how to avoid the problem next time.",
 "desc":"Can you charge more than you quoted? When a fixed quote binds you, when extra work justifies more, how to handle changes with a variation or new quote, and how to word quotes to allow for scope changes.",
 "lead":"The job turned out bigger than the quote &mdash; can you bill for the extra? Sometimes yes, sometimes no. It depends on <b>what you quoted and how you handled the change</b>. Here is how it works.",
 "sections":[
  {"h2":"A firm quote generally binds you","html":"<p>If you gave a <b>firm, fixed quote</b> and the client accepted it, that&rsquo;s the price for the work you described &mdash; you generally can&rsquo;t just bill more because it took longer. The fixed price is the deal. Charging above it without agreement is where disputes (and bad reviews) come from.</p>"},
  {"h2":"When you can charge more","html":"<p>You can charge more when the <b>work changed</b>: the client added scope, asked for extras, or the job turned out to be a different job than quoted. The test is whether the extra is <b>outside what you originally quoted for</b> &mdash; genuinely new work, not just the same work taking longer.</p>"},
  {"h2":"Handle it the right way","html":"<p>The moment you see the job exceeding the quote, <b>stop and tell the client</b> &mdash; before doing the extra work. Issue a <b>variation or a new quote</b> for the additional scope and get agreement. Billing a surprise at the end, even for legitimate extra work, feels like a bait-and-switch to the client.</p>"},
  {"h2":"Prevent it next time","html":"<p>Word your quotes to <b>define what&rsquo;s included and what counts as extra</b>, and add a line that changes to scope will be quoted separately. A tight scope and a change clause turn &ldquo;can I charge more?&rdquo; from an awkward argument into a simple, expected process.</p>"},
 ],
 "faq":[
  {"q":"Can I charge more than my quote if the job took longer?","a":"Generally no, if you gave a firm fixed quote for the work described &mdash; the same work simply taking longer is your risk. You can charge more when the scope actually changed or extra work was added."},
  {"q":"How do I bill for extra work beyond the quote?","a":"Tell the client before doing the extra work, issue a variation or new quote for the additional scope, and get agreement. Don't spring a surprise charge at the end, even for legitimate extras."},
  {"q":"How can I avoid quote overruns?","a":"Define clearly what the quote includes and what counts as extra, and add a line that any scope changes will be quoted separately. A tight scope makes extra charges expected rather than disputed."},
 ],
 "related":[{"href":"/en/estimate","label":"Create a quote"},{"href":"/en/blog/how-to-revise-a-quote","label":"How to revise a quote"},{"href":"/en/blog/fixed-price-vs-hourly-quote","label":"Fixed price vs hourly"}],
 "cta_href":"/en/estimate","cta_label":"Create a quote free",
},
